language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ ages_of_the_sons: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ An old man asked a mathematician to guess the ages of his three sons. The old man said: "The product of their ages is 36." The mathematician said: "I need more information." The old man said: "Over there you can see a building. The sum of their ages equals the number of the windows in that building." After a short while the mathematician said: "I need more information." The old man said: "The oldest son has blue eyes." The mathematician said: "I got it." What are the ages of the three sons of the old man?
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/ages_of_the_sons.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ A1, A2, A3: the ages of the three sons, from the oldest (A1) to the youngest (A3)
find A1, A2, A3 : int(1..36)

branching on [A1, A2, A3]

such that
$ the product of the three ages is 36
A1 * A2 * A3 = 36,
$ the ages are listed from the oldest to the youngest
A1 >= A2,
A2 >= A3,
$ every age is at least 1
A3 >= 1,
$ the oldest son has blue eyes, so the oldest son is unique: the two other sons are younger than him
A1 > A2,
$ the sum of the ages is the number of windows, and the mathematician still needs more information:
$ some other triple of ages with product 36 has the same sum
(exists b1, b2, b3 : int(1..36) .
    (b1 >= b2 /\ b2 >= b3 /\ b1 * b2 * b3 = 36 /\ b1 + b2 + b3 = A1 + A2 + A3 /\
     (b1 != A1 \/ b2 != A2 \/ b3 != A3))),
$ after the blue eyes clue the mathematician knows the ages: among the triples with product 36, the same sum
$ and a unique oldest son, the only one is the answer
forAll b1, b2, b3 : int(1..36) .
    ((b1 >= b2 /\ b2 >= b3 /\ b1 * b2 * b3 = 36 /\ b1 + b2 + b3 = A1 + A2 + A3 /\ b1 > b2 /\ b1 > b3)
     -> (b1 = A1 /\ b2 = A2 /\ b3 = A3))
